You are an R and Python programming assistant for **GastroDeconv2FateMap**.
Follow the practices below. Prefer British English in prose and documentation.

**Scope:** Do not add tasks (tests, docs, commits, refactors, or
enforcement of these rules in the codebase) unless requested; ask first.
Minimise scope: do not change unrelated code.

---

# Language-agnostic project structure and data

Inspired by
[Data Science Team Training — Data Organisation](https://dstt.stephenturner.us/dataorg.html#additional-best-practices)
and tailored from the DeCovarT package rules for this dual R/Python repo.

## Directory layout (this repository)

```
GastroDeconv2FateMap/
├── R/                 # package functions (reusable API)
├── scripts/           # R analysis / pipeline scripts (extensive use)
├── Python/            # Python analysis scripts (mirror numeric prefixes)
├── vignettes/         # Quarto vignettes → pkgdown articles
├── data/              # raw (immutable), intermediate, processed
│   ├── raw/
│   ├── intermediate/
│   └── processed/
├── outputs/           # figures, tables, rendered artefacts
├── article/           # manuscript materials (if any)
├── inst/              # package assets
├── man/               # roxygen-generated help
├── docs/              # pkgdown site build (GitHub Pages)
├── renv/ + renv.lock  # R dependency lock (always sync after installs)
├── pyproject.toml + uv.lock  # Python deps via uv (not Poetry/pip ad hoc)
└── _pkgdown.yml       # pkgdown navbar + articles index
```

- One project = one directory; use the `.Rproj` for the working directory.
- **Raw data is sacred:** never edit or overwrite `data/raw/`.
- All cleaning and derivation is scripted; intermediates are regenerated.
- Prefer plain-text analysis-ready tables (CSV/TSV/Parquet) over `.xlsx`.
- Version-control **code and small text**; do not commit large or binary
  raw data. Use a strict `.gitignore`.
- Track large raw files with **[DVC](https://dvc.org/)**; commit `.dvc`
  pointers, not blobs.
- Avoid `"final"` / `"FINAL_v3"` in file names; use Git history instead.
- Prefer Quarto for reproducible reports when available.

## File naming

- Short, descriptive **`snake_case`** (or `kebab-case` for dated outputs).
- No spaces or special characters (`@`, `#`, `%`, parentheses).
- Dated artefacts: `YYYY-MM-DD-descriptive-slug.ext`.
- Ordered scripts: zero-padded prefixes (`01_import_data.R`,
  `Python/06_01_….py` ↔ `scripts/06_01_….R` when mirrored).

## Diagrams and documentation visuals

- **Pipelines / architecture:** Mermaid (preferred in README / Quarto) or
  Excalidraw for free-form sketches.
- README badges: only stacks actually used (R, renv, uv, scanpy/Seurat,
  Quarto, pkgdown)—do not invent unused stacks.

---

# R — package, scripts, and renv

## Project roles

- Reusable package code lives in `R/`.
- Analysis / simulation scripts live in `scripts/` (this project uses
  scripts heavily; keep one-off logic there until reused twice).
- Vignettes live in `vignettes/` (Quarto).
- External package files live in `inst/`; read with `system.file()`.
- Manage R deps with **`renv`**: `renv::install()`, `renv::snapshot()`,
  commit `renv.lock`. Do not rely on ad-hoc library installs in CI;
  GitHub Actions currently use `setup-r-dependencies` with
  `RENV_CONFIG_AUTOLOADER_ENABLED=false`.

## Package structure

- Declare dependencies with versions in `DESCRIPTION`
  (e.g. `dplyr (>= 1.1.0)`).
- Keep `LICENSE` / `LICENSE.md` and `NEWS.md` up to date.
- Document with **roxygen2**; run `devtools::document()`.
- Before interactive testing: `devtools::load_all()`.
- Validate with `devtools::check()` when asked (notes OK; avoid
  warnings/errors). **Do not add or expand test suites / coverage
  unless the user asks.**

## Naming conventions

- `snake_case` for variables and functions.
- `UpperCamelCase` for R6, S3, S4, and S7 class names.
- `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants and global options.
- Prefix data-frame objects with `df_` when it aids clarity.
- Use verbs for functions; avoid ambiguous names (`customer_id`, not `id`).
- Do not shadow R builtins (`sd`, `data`, etc.).
- R6 private methods and fields start with `.`.

## Coding style

- Follow the [tidyverse style guide](https://style.tidyverse.org/).
- Prefer `dplyr::mutate(..., .keep = "none")` over superseded
  `transmute()`.
- Use the native pipe `|>`.
- Use `package::function()` for non-base functions.
- Use `glue::glue()` for string interpolation.
- 2-space indentation; keep lines ≤ 80 characters.
- Assign with `<-`; use `=` only inside R6 methods.
- Handle missing values explicitly (`na.rm = TRUE`, `is.na()`).
- Comment non-obvious logic only.
- Prefer modular, reusable functions over long scripts.
- Save ggplot2 figures with `ggplot2::ggsave()`, not base `grDevices`,
  unless TikZ/LaTeX export is required.

### dplyr programming (indirection)

- Data-masking from the caller: embrace with `{{ var }}`.
- Character names: `.data[[var]]` / `.data[["column"]]`.
- Tidy-select with characters: `dplyr::all_of()` / `any_of()`.

**Tidyverse preferences:** `lubridate`, `stringr`, `forcats`,
`dplyr` / `data.table` (match the script’s existing style).

## Performance

- Profile with `profvis` before optimising.
- Prefer vectorised code, apply family, or `purrr` over loops.
- `data.table` for large in-memory tables; `duckdb` for out-of-memory.
- CSV/TSV: `readr` with `show_col_types = FALSE`, or `data.table::fread()`.

## Preferred R dependencies

| Task | Package |
|------|---------|
| Lists / functional code | `purrr` |
| Paths | `here`, `fs` |
| Tabular data | `dplyr`, `data.table` |
| Out-of-memory data | `duckdb` |
| Plotting | `ggplot2`; interactive: `ggiraph` |
| Input checks | `checkmate` |
| Messages | `cli` |
| Strings | `glue` |
| Parallelism | `mirai` / existing `furrr` patterns |
| Reproducibility | `renv`, Quarto |
| Package workflow | `devtools`, `usethis`, `roxygen2` |
| Website | `pkgdown` |

